            <h3>Choosing the Right LLM for Each Task</h3>
            <p>Picking one model for everything is simple, and it is also the quickest way to overspend or underdeliver. A better habit is to match each request to the smallest model that does the job well, and reserve the big models for the work that needs them.</p>
            <h4>Start with the shape of the task</h4>
            <ul>
            <li><b>Formatting, classification, extraction:</b> short, well specified jobs. A fast, low cost model handles these reliably.</li>
            <li><b>Chat and drafting:</b> a balanced model gives good tone and speed.</li>
            <li><b>Coding:</b> small diffs suit mid size models. Architecture questions and hard debugging reward stronger reasoning.</li>
            <li><b>Planning, math, hard decisions:</b> choose a reasoning model and give it room to think.</li>
            <li><b>Images and screenshots:</b> choose a model with vision input.</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Read the catalogue numbers</h4>
            <p>Every model in our <a href="/models">catalogue</a> lists its context window and its price per million input and output tokens. For example, <code>gpt-4o-mini</code> is listed at $0.15 input and $0.60 output with a 128K window, while <code>gpt-5.4</code> is listed at $2.50 and $15.00 with a 1M window. Output tokens usually cost more than input tokens, so tasks that return short answers over long inputs are the cheapest to run.</p>
            <h4>Let the auto profiles route for you</h4>
            <p>Text Generator ships semantic auto models. Set the model name once and each request is routed by what it asks for:</p>
            <ul>
            <li><code>text-generator/auto</code> balances quality, speed, and cost.</li>
            <li><code>text-generator/auto-fast</code> targets interactive assistants and quick agent loops.</li>
            <li><code>text-generator/auto-cheap</code> targets formatting, classification, and simple edits.</li>
            <li><code>text-generator/auto-code</code> routes coding prompts by complexity.</li>
            <li><code>text-generator/auto-reasoning</code> picks the model and thinking level for hard problems.</li>
            <li><code>text-generator/auto-vision</code> handles image understanding and OCR.</li>
            </ul>
            <pre><code class="language-python">from openai import OpenAI
            
            client = OpenAI(base_url="https://api.text-generator.io/v1", api_key=API_KEY)
            r = client.chat.completions.create(
                model="text-generator/auto-cheap",
                messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Label this ticket as billing, bug, or feature: 'My invoice shows two charges'"}],
            )
            print(r.choices[0].message.content)</code></pre>
            <h4>A simple evaluation loop</h4>
            <ol>
            <li>Collect 20 real prompts from your product.</li>
            <li>Run them through the cheap profile and the balanced profile.</li>
            <li>Compare answers side by side and note where the cheaper model already meets your bar.</li>
            <li>Move that task type to the cheaper profile and keep the stronger one for the rest.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>Repeat when new models arrive. Because the API is OpenAI compatible, switching is a one line change to the model name.</p>
            <h4>Keep fallbacks in mind</h4>
            <p>Catalogue entries list fallback models, so a request can continue on a similar model if a provider has trouble. Set a generous <code>max_tokens</code> for long outputs and log which model served each request, so your evaluation data stays current.</p>
